898a4-cf36b | 2026-06-29T11:10:45Z | session:898a41b5-68e0-4b0f-b16c-c6072454bd6a | Assistant: Good question — "door" is just the metaphor for **the app-facing API surface**. The refactor splits the public surface into two doors: \n  \n - **Read door** = how an app *consumes* data → typed read sessions ("given the store, what should the UI see?"). \n - **Write door** = how an app *produces and emits* Nostr events → everything an app does that creates a new event on the network. \n  \n So "write what?" — **Nostr events.** Every user action that isn't just reading is a write: post a note, reply, react/like, repost, follow/unfollow, edit profile (kind:0), send a DM, join/post in a group, delete an event, mute someone. All of those are "writes" the app makes through this door.
898a4-0e196 | 2026-06-29T11:10:45Z | session:898a41b5-68e0-4b0f-b16c-c6072454bd6a | - **Write door** = how an app *produces and emits* Nostr events → everything an app does that creates a new event on the network. \n  \n So "write what?" — **Nostr events.** Every user action that isn't just reading is a write: post a note, reply, react/like, repost, follow/unfollow, edit profile (kind:0), send a DM, join/post in a group, delete an event, mute someone. All of those are "writes" the app makes through this door.
898a4-a8ec9 | 2026-06-29T11:10:45Z | session:898a41b5-68e0-4b0f-b16c-c6072454bd6a | Assistant: At a high level, a write in NMP is a **typed action that flows as bytes into the Rust kernel, which constructs → signs → publishes the event and streams status back out** — with the app shell owning none of that logic. Here's the pipeline, using "post a note" as the running example. \n  \n ### The flow \n  \n **1. App states intent as a typed action (not raw bytes).** \n The shell (Swift/Kotlin/TS) calls a *generated builder* — e.g. `nip01.publish_note { content, tags }`. It never hand-rolls an event or says "publish these bytes." Each action has an `ACTION_CONTRACT` entry, so the set of legal writes is typed and codegen-checked. This is the "no generic `PublishRaw`" rule (WRITE-001). \n  \n **2. It crosses the one byte doorway.** \n The action is encoded into a `DispatchEnvelope` (FlatBuffers) and pushed through `dispatch_action` — the *single* command lane (legacy decision 0064). This is the only FFI seam, and it's identical in shape across native (C-ABI today, UniFFI next) and browser (wasm). The JSON doorway was deleted; it's typed bytes only. \n  \n **3. The kernel actor routes it to the right ActionModule.** \n `nmp-core` (the actor runtime + state machine) receives the envelope and dispatches to the per-NIP adapter that owns that action — here the NIP-01 `ActionModule`. `ActionModule::execute()` is the *sole* write path (legacy decision 0027). Everything from here is Rust-owned and runs inside the actor, single-threaded per the kernel's model. \n  \n **4. A local publish intent is created immediately — before anything touches the network.** \n The first thing the kernel does is mint a **status fact** owned by Rust: `pending`. This is the offline-first root — the write exists and is durable *before* a signer or relay is involved. This is why "dispatch ≠ success": the app gets a real status object, not a fire-and-forget. \n  \n **5. Construction.** \n The ActionModule builds the *unsigned* event (kind:1, tags, content, timestamps). Still no signing, no network. \n  \n **6. Signing — a generic capability, backend invisible.** \n The kernel invokes the signing `CapabilityModule`. Crucially the action doesn't know or care *how* it's signed — local nsec, NIP-46 bunker (remote, async round-trip over the shared relay lane), or hardware. The signer backend is deliberately invisible to the protocol worker. Status advances `pending → signed`. (This is the "signing and publishing are orthogonal" doctrine made structural — and WRITE-003's typed `SignerProvenance` is what names *which* identity is signing, with a structured error if it's unknown.) \n  \n **7. Publishing — a separate, routed, provenance-classified step.** \n Now the publish planner decides *where* this goes and **classifies the route by provenance** (ADR-0071): a normal note routes to the author's outbox relays (kind:10002), a group event routes by its `h`-tag relays, a DM by the recipient's inbox, etc. Status advances `signed → stored → planned → sent`. The route can't be anonymous/defaulted — provenance must be explicit (that's what WRITE-004 closes). \n  \n **8. Status streams back out the read door — reactively, no polling.** \n As relay `OK`/`AUTH`/error responses arrive, the kernel advances the status fact to terminal states (`sent`, `failed`, `exhausted`) and pushes those out as `ViewBatch` snapshots. The shell *observes* the write's status the same way it observes any read — via pushed snapshots, never by polling. So the UI shows "sending… / published / failed" off real Rust-owned state. \n  \n ### The shape of it \n ``` \n shell: typed action builder ─┐ \n                              ▼  (FlatBuffers DispatchEnvelope) \n                     one byte doorway (dispatch_action) \n                              ▼ \n    nmp-core actor ──► ActionModule::execute()  (per-NIP, Rust-owned) \n                              │ \n         ┌────────────────────┼─────────────────────┐ \n         ▼                    ▼                       ▼ \n    mint intent          construct                 sign (capability, \n    (status: pending)    unsigned event            backend-invisible) \n                              │                       │ \n                              └──────────┬────────────┘ \n                                         ▼ \n                           publish planner (route by provenance) \n                                         ▼ \n                             shared relay lane ──► OK/AUTH \n                                         ▼ \n                      status fact advances ─► ViewBatch snapshot ─► shell renders \n ```
898a4-7ff83 | 2026-06-29T11:10:45Z | session:898a41b5-68e0-4b0f-b16c-c6072454bd6a | ### Why it needed its own redesign \n The core doctrine here (one of the owner's standing rules) is **signing and publishing must be orthogonal** — and the old surface conflated a bunch of distinct concepts into loose, ambiguous calls. The write door's job is to cleanly separate the three things that happen when an app emits an event: \n  \n 1. **Construction** — build the event (kind, tags, content). "What is this event?" \n 2. **Signing** — a *generic capability* (local key, bunker/NIP-46, hardware). "Who attests to it?" \n 3. **Publishing** — a *separate, optional, routed* step. "Where does it go, and via what provenance?" \n  \n Plus two invariants threaded through all of it: \n - **Provenance** — every publish must declare *why/how* it's being routed (explicit route classification), not just "send these bytes somewhere." \n - **Dispatch ≠ success** — handing an event to the publish pipeline is not the same as it landing. Rust owns a **local publish intent / status fact** (pending → signed → stored → planned → sent → failed/exhausted), so the app gets honest, offline-first write state instead of optimistic fire-and-forget. \n
