898a4-4c843 | 2026-06-29T11:10:45Z | session:898a41b5-68e0-4b0f-b16c-c6072454bd6a | - **Provenance** — every publish must declare *why/how* it's being routed (explicit route classification), not just "send these bytes somewhere." \n - **Dispatch ≠ success** — handing an event to the publish pipeline is not the same as it landing. Rust owns a **local publish intent / status fact** (pending → signed → stored → planned → sent → failed/exhausted), so the app gets honest, offline-first write state instead of optimistic fire-and-forget. \n  [...] **7. Publishing — a separate, routed, provenance-classified step.** \n Now the publish planner decides *where* this goes and **classifies the route by provenance** (ADR-0071): a normal note routes to the author's outbox relays (kind:10002), a group event routes by its `h`-tag relays, a DM by the recipient's inbox, etc. Status advances `signed → stored → planned → sent`. The route can't be anonymous/defaulted — provenance must be explicit (that's what WRITE-004 closes). \n  [...] - **WRITE-004** (open, no PR) — delete the *anonymous route default* (`Default for PublishRouteClass`), so a publish can never silently route without declared provenance. \n - **WRITE-005** (PR #2398, red) — restrict *pre-signed verbatim publish* (publishing an already-signed event without re-signing) to protocol-owned seams like Marmot/relay-owned events — it's not a general app write door.
898a4-5df19 | 2026-06-29T11:10:45Z | session:898a41b5-68e0-4b0f-b16c-c6072454bd6a | - **WRITE-001** ✅ — stop handing apps a generic `PublishRaw` ("publish any bytes") as the default door; force typed, intentful writes. \n - **WRITE-002** ✅ — make publish finalization a *named pre-sign gate* (enforces dispatch ≠ success). \n - **WRITE-003** ✅ — replace a loose optional `signer_pubkey` with a typed `SignerProvenance` ("which identity is signing, and is it known?"), with structured failure for unknown signers. \n - **WRITE-004** (open, no PR) — delete the *anonymous route default* (`Default for PublishRouteClass`), so a publish can never silently route without declared provenance. \n - **WRITE-005** (PR #2398, red) — restrict *pre-signed verbatim publish* (publishing an already-signed event without re-signing) to protocol-owned seams like Marmot/relay-owned events — it's not a general app write door. \n  [...] There *is* a low-level generic publish seam (`PublishRaw`/`ProtocolCommand`) — it's in the canonical list of seams you must prove can't already express a need before adding anything to NMP. What WRITE-001 did was stop the **DX/starter docs from teaching `PublishRaw` as the normal way to write**. So the intended path for your made-up kind is *a typed app-owned builder*, not raw bytes — same typed, intentful shape as a built-in NIP action, just owned by your app. Raw publish remains underneath as the generic substrate the builder is built on. \n  [...] - **#2369** SLICE-NS-WRITE-002: make publish finalization a named pre-sign gate — pre-sign gate step named and enforced in write path. \n - **#2368** SLICE-NS-WRITE-001: stop DX path from teaching generic PublishRaw — PublishRaw banned from starter/DX paths; explicit typed routes enforced. [...] The kernel invokes the signing `CapabilityModule`. Crucially the action doesn't know or care *how* it's signed — local nsec, NIP-46 bunker (remote, async round-trip over the shared relay lane), or hardware. The signer backend is deliberately invisible to the protocol worker. Status advances `pending → signed`. (This is the "signing and publishing are orthogonal" doctrine made structural — and WRITE-003's typed `SignerProvenance` is what names *which* identity is signing, with a structured error if it's unknown.) \n  [...]  \n The one *special-case* door is **pre-signed verbatim publish** (`publish_signed_event`): publish an already-signed event without re-signing, routed via the event's own pubkey outbox. That's needed for protocol-owned events (e.g. Marmot/MLS wire events) — and WRITE-005 (#2398, the red PR) is what's restricting it to those protocol seams so it can't be used as a general app write door. \n
3c942-3b05e | 2026-06-29T11:24:16Z | session:3c942260-311d-4e00-8bcc-204045ea87b3 | Assistant: Codex feedback is high-quality — **shape approved** ("the right UniFFI pattern"), with concrete, valid refinements. Key takeaways: \n  \n - **Crate:** `crates/nmp-uniffi`, **in-workspace** (the #2149 out-of-workspace was app-specific, not a real constraint); bindgen behind a feature/bin, out of normal `cargo build`. \n - **Blocking:** `DispatchOutcome` must carry `{correlation_id, error, code}` — coded rejections are load-bearing in tests, my 2-case enum dropped `code`. \n - **High:** don't wrap `nmp-ffi`/parse JSON — **move the FFI-free dispatch core into `nmp-native-runtime`** and wrap that. [...] - **Crate:** `crates/nmp-uniffi`, **in-workspace** (the #2149 out-of-workspace was app-specific, not a real constraint); bindgen behind a feature/bin, out of normal `cargo build`. \n - **Blocking:** `DispatchOutcome` must carry `{correlation_id, error, code}` — coded rejections are load-bearing in tests, my 2-case enum dropped `code`. \n - **High:** don't wrap `nmp-ffi`/parse JSON — **move the FFI-free dispatch core into `nmp-native-runtime`** and wrap that. [...] User: [Agent task result: Dynamic workflow "Implement + review the #2389 UniFFI app-lifecycle/byte-doorway scaffold (foundational)" completed] \n {"code":{"opened_pr":true,"pr_url":"https://github.com/pablof7z/nostr-multi-platform/pull/2397","branch":"ns-2389-uniffi-scaffold","summary":"New crate nmp-uniffi (#2389): UniFFI 0.29 proc-macro bindings wrapping nmp-native-runtime. Exposes NmpApp (Arc-wrapped), UpdateSink callback interface (quiescence-safe), and DispatchOutcome record. FFI-free dispatch core moved to nmp-native-runtime::action_dispatch returning typed DispatchOutcome; C-ABI and UniFFI both consume the same implementation. Fixed a NmpApp::shutdown() deadlock where actor_starter's captured update_tx kept the listener thread alive forever when the actor was never started. Generated Swift/Kotlin bindings checked in under crates/nmp-uniffi/generated/ with CI drift gate.","dispatch_core_moved":"Moved from crates/nmp-ffi/src/action/bytes.rs to crates/nmp-native-runtime/src/action_dispatch.rs as pub fn dispatch_action_bytes_typed(app: &NmpApp, bytes: &[u8]) -> DispatchOutcome. The C-ABI nmp-ffi::action::bytes::dispatch_action_bytes is now a thin wrapper calling dispatch_action_bytes_typed and converting to JSON. nmp-uniffi::NmpApp::dispatch_action calls dispatch_action_bytes_typed directly. The C-ABI still passes (cargo test -p nmp-ffi: all green).","tests_added":["crates/nmp-native-runtime/src/action_dispatch_tests.rs: malformed_envelope_produces_error_outcome, oversize_bytes_produce_error_outcome, dispatch_preserves_host_supplied_correlation_id, coded_rejection_carries_both_error_and_code, plain_rejection_has_error_but_no_code, unknown_namespace_produces_error_outcome","crates/nmp-uniffi/src/tests.rs: clamp_visible_zero_yields_default, clamp_visible_clamps_to_range, clamp_emit_hz_zero_yields_default, clamp_emit_hz_clamps_to_range, sink_register_and_clear, sink_replace_is_atomic, sink_panicking_callback_is_contained, clear_waits_for_in_flight_callback, shutdown_during_in_flight_update_no_uaf_no_deadlock, dispatch_malformed_envelope_produces_error_outcome, dispatch_wrapper_passes_through_correlation_id, dispatch_wrapper_passes_through_code_field"],"tests_run":["cargo test -p nmp-uniffi: 12/12 passed","cargo test -p nmp-native-runtime: 77/77 passed (71 unit + 1 integration + 5 doc-tests)","cargo test -p nmp-ffi: all passed","cargo test -p nmp-testing --test doctrine_lint_smoke: 127/127 passed"],"all_green":true,"notes":"Key implementation detail: UniFFI 0.28/0.29 generates FfiConverter impls for Box<dyn Trait> (not Arc<dyn Trait>) for callback interfaces. UpdateSink parameter in set_update_sink uses Box<dyn UpdateSink>; internally converted to Arc<dyn UpdateSink> via Arc::from(s) so the closure satisfies UpdateListener = Arc<dyn Fn + Send + Sync>. The shutdown() deadlock was caused by actor_starter holding the original update_tx clone: the listener thread's while let Ok(update) = update_rx.recv() loop would never exit unless both update_tx clones (actor_starter's + startup_update_tx) were dropped. Fixed by adding actor_starter.take() to shutdown(). The uniffi 'bindgen' feature was renamed to 'cli' in 0.29 for the uniffi_bindgen_main entrypoint. Cargo.toml uses version = '0.29' which resolved to 0.29.5."},"review":{"verdict":"request_changes","quiescence_sound":true,"spec_conformant":true,"findings":[{"severity":"major","issue":"crates/nmp-uniffi/src/tests.rs:222-232 `shutdown_during_in_flight_update_no_uaf_no_deadlock` does NOT create an in-flight update. It installs a non-blocking RecordSink and calls shutdown() (then again for idempotency) with no callback executing. The single highest-risk behavior the spec demanded be PROVEN — shutdown joining the listener thread WHILE a foreign on_update is mid-flight — is never exercised; the test only proves shutdown is callable and idempotent. The name overstates coverage. Fix: install a barrier-blocking sink, fire the passive pre-start snapshot so on_update is genuinely in-flight, call shutdown() on the main thread, release the barrier from a helper thread, and assert shutdown() returns (join completes) without deadlock/UAF."},{"severity":"major","issue":"crates/nmp-uniffi/src/tests.rs:170-219 `clear_waits_for_in_flight_callback` — the canonical quiescence proof — is sleep/timing-based (main sleeps 2ms then clears; worker sleeps 5ms then releases the barrier) and asserts nothing. If the listener thread processes the passive snapshot AFTER clear() sets listener=None, on_update never runs, the Barrier(2) only gets the worker's wait(), and worker.join() hangs forever (no test timeout). It also never asserts that clear() actually blocked on in_flight>0. Make it deterministic: signal callback entry via a channel/Condvar before clearing, and assert the wait happened, instead of racing on sleeps."},{"severity":"minor","issue":"crates/nmp-uniffi/src/tests.rs:148-159 `sink_panicking_callback_is_contained` does not isolate the wrapper's panic containment. The panic fires on the runtime listener thread, which already wraps the callback in its own catch_unwind (crates/nmp-native-runtime/src/app_ctor.rs:294). The test would still pass if the wrapper's catch_unwind (crates/nmp-uniffi/src/lib.rs:153) were deleted, so it does not actually guard the behavior it claims."},{"severity":"nit","issue":"Stale UniFFI version in docs: crates/nmp-uniffi/src/lib.rs:139 and crates/nmp-uniffi/src/tests.rs:14 say 'UniFFI 0.28' while Cargo.toml:37 pins uniffi = \"0.29\". The Box<dyn UpdateSink> lift type is correct for 0.29 callback_interface; only the comments are wrong and could mislead future maintainers."},{"severity":"nit","issue":"Reentrancy is documented as forbidden (crates/nmp-uniffi/src/lib.rs:57-59) but not tested. Acceptable since a true reentrancy test deadlocks by design, but the spec's 'forbidden + proven' bar leaves this as an asserted-only contract. Consider a timeout-guarded or documented-no-test rationale. Also note: the generated Kotlin class is AutoCloseable/Disposable (nmp_uniffi.kt:1475) with a UniFFI-provided close() distinct from the logical shutdown(); design is consistent (close() frees the Arc and Drop calls shutdown() as fallback), but hosts must call shutdown() for logical teardown."}],"summary":"Architecture, build wiring, and the quiescence MECHANISM are sound. The pre-existing UpdateListenerGate Condvar drain is consumed correctly: set_update_sink clear/replace route through the draining set_update_listener (lib.rs:157); &[u8] is copied to Vec<u8> BEFORE on_update (lib.rs:149); no wrapper lock is held across the foreign call; the call is catch_unwind-wrapped (lib.rs:153); and shutdown()'s ordering (clear listener -> drop both update senders -> join actor -> join listener) is UAF/deadlock-free by construction, including the never-started-app case the PR fixes. The FFI-free dispatch core was genuinely moved to nmp-native-runtime::action_dispatch::dispatch_action_bytes_typed; the C-ABI is now a JSON-serialization shim (outcome_to_json) preserving byte-identical {correlation_id}/{error}/{error,code} output and its null-app + oversize guards, with dispatch_action_json/finish_dispatch/rejection_json correctly gated behind test/test-support so normal builds don't break. DispatchOutcome carries correlation_id/error/code; clamps (0->100/1..500, 0->6/1..12) are preserved with parity tests; crate is in-workspace; bindgen is behind the bindgen feature + required-features bin (out of default build, no build.rs); Swift/Kotlin bindings are checked in with a CI drift gate and added to .file-size-ignore; shutdown is explicit, idempotent, and named shutdown (not close); files are under the 500-LOC gate. The blocking issue is test proof, not correctness: the spec emphatically required the hard behaviors to be PROVEN by tests, but the highest-risk one — shutdown during a genuinely in-flight callback — is not exercised (uses a non-blocking sink), and the quiescence-clear test is sleep-based with a latent hang and no assertions. Request changes to make  \n ... (truncated 139 chars, full result in /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-pablofernandez-Work-nostr-multi-platform/3c942260-311d-4e00-8bcc-204045ea87b3/tasks/w5awq38hn.output) [...] **`ActionResultObserver` — stop-and-report:**   \n `ActionRegistry::deliver_result` holds the `Arc<Mutex>` lock ACROSS the observer call (mutual-exclusion quiescence, not Condvar+in_flight drain). No `clear_result_observer` API exists on the registry. Per the task spec: this is the stop-and-report condition. Registration and replacement are safe (mutex exclusion ensures the old observer finished before the new one is installed), but Barrier-style teardown drain tests are absent. A follow-up issue should add a drain gate to `ActionRegistry` before M14-D deletes the C-ABI. \n  \n ## Quiescence / teardown tests \n  \n 16 new tests. The `CapabilitySink` quiescence suite includes: \n - `capability_sink_clear_waits_for_in_flight` — full Barrier proof: `callback_done` (set inside the callback AFTER the gate) must be `true` when `set_capability_callback(None)` returns. \n - `capability_sink_shutdown_during_in_flight_no_uaf` — no UAF/deadlock on shutdown with in-flight callback. \n  \n All tests: `cargo test -p nmp-uniffi` (88 pass), `cargo test -p nmp-core` (green), `cargo test -p nmp-ffi` (green), doctrine lint smoke (129 pass), bindings drift (OK). \n  \n Assistant: C4 complete (PR #2428, 6 methods) — and the agent correctly hit a real **stop-and-report**: `CapabilitySink` got full Condvar+in_flight quiescence, but **`ActionRegistry` has no drain/clear gate** for the result observer (it holds a mutex across the call; the C-ABI shares this gap, so it's not a regression — but there's no clean teardown-with-drain). That's a genuine finding I need to track as an **M14-D blocker** before the C-ABI is deleted. Filing it, then dispatching C5: \n  \n Assistant: #2429 filed as the M14-D blocker. Dispatching **C5 — feed/search/URI sessions**, stacked on C4: \n  \n Assistant: C4 done (PR #2428) and its real finding tracked as **#2429** (ActionRegistry drain-gate, blocks M14-D). C5 (feed/search/URI sessions) is building, stacked on C4.
