91a86-f4fd1 | 2026-07-03T09:34:04Z | session:91a86fdf-624c-446e-9b38-0fb02085121f | **Spending is a durable state machine, because two things can fail independently** — the mint HTTP call and the relay publish. A per-operation journal (`Draft → MintPending → MintSettled → PublishPending → Settled`, with `Unknown`/`Failed`) means a process crash mid-operation reconciles on restart instead of double-spending. [...] ## No — don't build the wallet money-safety journal on Trellis. (The read projection already rides it.) \n  \n **The one fact that settles it:** Trellis graphs are **in-memory, per-session, and die with the process by design** — built fresh in `FeedSessionTrellisAdapter::new`, torn down on `close_scope`, with zero persistence in `trellis-core`. The wallet journal's *entire reason to exist* is surviving process death after an irreversible external effect (a mint spend). Trellis can't even represent that problem, let alone solve it. [...] **What to use instead — no new substrate needed.** There's no generic durable-saga crate in the workspace, and building one now would be a one-consumer abstraction. The design doc already specifies the right shape: `nmp-wallet` owns an **actor-owned durable journal** persisted through NMP storage, with command-shaped reducers that never await mint HTTP, capability-lane mint workers returning raw results, and the existing publish engine handling `PublishPending`. That's the standard NMP actor pattern + a persisted journal. (If a second saga ever appears — `nmp-marmot`'s pending-MLS-autopublish is the likely candidate — extract a generic saga substrate *then*, post-hoc.)
91a86-4c25c | 2026-07-03T09:34:04Z | session:91a86fdf-624c-446e-9b38-0fb02085121f | **So the clean way to get concern 3: event-source the wallet reducer.** Make current state a fold over an ordered stream of typed facts — `TokenAdded`, `TokenDeleted{cause}`, `MintProbeReconciled{proof, verdict}`, `NutzapRedeemed`, etc. — and have each fold step retain a bounded in-memory ring of "what changed + why." Then: \n - **Current state** = the fold result (concern 2), \n - **"Why is it this shape"** = replay the retained delta ring (concern 3), \n - and neither of those is your money-safety journal (concern 1), which persists separately with different correctness rules. [...] **The key distinction: an event log is not a dependency graph.** Your question — "what sequence produced the current state?" — is answered by an **ordered causal timeline**. Trellis answers a *different* question: "what does the current state *depend on*, and what recomputes when X changes?" A dependency graph. For "why is it this shape," a linear annotated log is the more direct *and* simpler primitive than bending Trellis's graph/trace machinery to narrate itself. [...] **On Trellis for concern 3 specifically:** it's a genuinely *closer* fit here than for the saga — this part is read-side, in-memory-OK, and Trellis really does have trace/replay. But I'd still not make it the driver, for two reasons: (a) the *explanation* is a timeline, and event-sourcing gives you that more cleanly than a graph trace; (b) the wallet-specific causal semantics — *why* a deletion fired, what a mint probe *concluded* — are Nostr/product meaning that ADR-0075 keeps in NMP, not in Trellis core. If the wallet projection already rides Trellis-backed session reconciliation, let its trace hooks surface the plumbing invisibly — but the causal vocabulary lives in `nmp-wallet`. [...] The streams aren't distinguished by *lifetime* — they're distinguished by **write moment**: \n  \n - **Saga (1)** writes **pre-effect**: "about to consume proofs A,B for a mint spend." That pre-record *is* the at-most-once mechanism. Durable, money-critical. \n - **Trail (3)** writes **post-observation**: "a deletion arrived / a probe found C spent / a nutzap redeemed." Diagnostic, bounded. \n - **State (2)** is the fold over the post-observation facts. [...] The load-bearing correction: **you can't read 2/3 out of 1**, because the saga only knows *locally-initiated* operations — and most of what makes the wallet chaotic (inbound token arrivals, NIP-09 deletions from your *other* devices, incoming nutzaps) never touches the saga at all. So it's not a subset relationship. It's **producer/consumer**: the saga *emits facts into* the trail (`MintSettled`, `Unknown→reconciled`, `Failed`) so the trail can explain locally-caused shape changes too — but their schemas never merge (money-critical pre-effect records must not live in a diagnostic log with bounded eviction). [...] I had said "in-memory, rebuild on restart." Fable caught that the naive rebuild is weak: after restart, every pre-session cause collapses to "this is just what the relays said" — you lose exactly the explanation you wanted ("balance dropped because last session a mint probe found spent proofs"). But the durable causal tier **already exists in the protocol**: NIP-60 `kind:7376` history (which the design doc already *requires* for every balance-changing op, and `nmp-nip60/src/history_event.rs` already has the codec) plus the `del` field on token events. So: \n  \n - **Don't build a durable local trail.** `kind:7376` + `del` tags *are* the durable, cross-device, encrypted causal record. \n - **The in-memory ring is the session-local high-resolution overlay** — mint-probe verdicts, arrival provenance, saga correlation ids — the stuff too fine-grained or too transient for 7376. \n - **Restart** = fold 7376 into coarse pre-session facts, then the ring accumulates fine facts from there. Two resolutions, one timeline. [...] ```rust \n // The fact stream — post-observation, each carrying WHY + provenance. \n enum WalletFact { \n     TokenAdded     { token_event: EventId, amount: Msat, mint: MintUrl, via: Provenance }, \n     TokenDeleted   { token_event: EventId, cause: DeleteCause },      // NIP-09 in, or local rollover \n     MintProbed     { proof: ProofRef, verdict: ProofVerdict },        // Spent / Unspent / Unknown \n     NutzapRedeemed { nutzap: EventId, amount: Msat, sender: PubkeyRef }, \n     SagaTransition { op: CorrelationId, from: OpState, to: OpState },  // producer wiring from concern 1 \n     StateRebuilt   { from: Vec<EventId> },                            // genesis fact after restart \n } \n  \n enum Provenance { Relay(RelayRef), Saga(CorrelationId), MintRollover } \n enum DeleteCause { Nip09Delete { by: EventId }, LocalRollover { op: CorrelationId } } [...] Fold it two ways: \n  \n - **The ring** (time-ordered, bounded) → answers *"what sequence produced this shape?"* \n - **A per-atom last-cause index** (`token_event_id → last WalletFact that touched it`) → answers *"why is **this specific** proof/token here?"* This is O(current state), not O(traffic), so a nutzap flood can't evict the cause of a token you still hold. Fable's addition — and it's the half of your question the ring alone can't answer. \n  \n Four rules that keep it honest: \n  \n 1. **The reducer must be confluent** — terminal state order-*insensitive* (a delete arriving before its token must tombstone, not no-op), even though the trail is order-*sensitive*. Otherwise two devices show different balances and the trail "explains" a state that shouldn't exist. \n 2. **The ring is never a rebuild authority** — restart rebuilds from Nostr + saga reconcile, entering as a `StateRebuilt` genesis fact. (Name it explicitly so nobody later "optimizes" restart into ring-replay.) \n 3. **Concern 2 "invisibly on Trellis" applies to *acquisition only*** (which relays/filters, per `kind:10019`) — never to proof-set derivation, which is NMP/actor-owned product meaning (ADR-0075 Ownership). \n 4. **Privacy at the type level** — fact payloads carry event ids, op ids, amounts-by-unit, and canonical mint URLs only when already public via `kind:10019`; never proofs or secrets. Bake it into the types, not a display-edge filter. [...] And on Trellis, the question that started this: doubly moot for the trail — a timeline isn't a dependency graph, *and* ADR-0075 confines Trellis trace to dev-only tooling while "why is my wallet this shape" is a product-surface question. \n  \n Net design in one line: **`nmp-wallet` owns an event-sourced, confluent reducer; state is the fold, the causal trail is a bounded in-memory ring + a per-atom cause index over the same facts, the saga feeds facts in as a producer, and `kind:7376` is the durable tier — all separate from the money-safety journal's persisted at-most-once records.**
