dcc80-57da7 | 2026-07-03T09:41:31Z | session:dcc80382-bcc0-45ea-8b9c-1a2fc741f872 | ## The boundary in the other direction — don't over-abstract \n  \n The engine owns the common spine, not everything. Feeds have windowing, paging (`load_more`), and root-admission scope compilation that replies will never need; search has relay pinning that feeds don't. Those stay concept-specific — model them as optional capabilities on top of the spine, not slots in a god-trait that every concept must stub out. ADR-0069's endorsement of verbose composition applies: this is factoring of *mechanics*, not a framework DSL. A reasonable practical shape is to extract the concept-neutral part of `nmp-feed-session`'s engine (demand registration + replay-before-live + live sink + registry/teardown) into an internal concept-neutral module, keep feed as its first and most demanding client, land the four #2758 reads as thin specs on it, and migrate search's registry semantics onto it opportunistically rather than as a forced rewrite. [...]  \n ```text id="gcmmqu" \n There is already more than one read lifecycle lane: \n - feed session engine \n - NIP-50 search lifecycle \n - NIP-29/group-feed lifecycle \n - plus the deprecated observed feed-source escape hatch \n ``` [...] ```text id="lp9t82" \n The four new reads must be thin concept specs over shared lifecycle machinery. \n  \n A PR adding open_replies/open_reactions/open_reposts/open_zaps must not introduce four independent registries, four close maps, four replay implementations, or four teardown recipes. \n ``` [...] The concept owner may define: \n  \n ```text id="lm378m" \n target/spec type \n event demand compiler \n admission predicate \n event reducer \n typed output encoder \n concept-specific validation \n concept-specific tests \n ``` [...] The shared engine must own: \n  \n ```text id="ccotsl" \n handle allocation \n open/replace/close registry \n replay-before-live ordering \n live activation \n exact-demand withdrawal \n reverse teardown ordering \n output clear/tombstone \n account/source switch behavior \n coalesced output emission \n leak instrumentation \n ``` [...] ```text id="ejsu4b" \n 1. Extract the concept-neutral lifecycle spine from feed/group observed machinery into an internal module. \n  \n 2. Make feed remain the first heavy client. \n  \n 3. Implement one #2758 read, preferably reactions or replies, as the proof. \n  \n 4. Implement the other three #2758 reads as near-declarative specs. \n  \n 5. Add a ratchet/test: concept read modules cannot call raw open_interest/open_observed_projection/close_interest directly except inside the shared engine. \n  \n 6. Migrate search/group-feed later only where the new engine actually fits. \n ``` [...] The one-sentence constraint is good: \n  \n ```text id="dft3sp" \n The four #2758 reads are acceptance tests for the concept-read engine boundary: if any of them contains lifecycle code, the engine is incomplete. \n ``` \n  \n Assistant: You're right to be suspicious, and I want to sharpen exactly what's fishy about it before we act: the agent conflated a **stage variation** with a **lane exemption**. Let me confirm the current state of #2758 and the group-feed lane, then act on it. \n  \n Assistant: Done. Your suspicion was correct, and it's now encoded as doctrine rather than left to the agents' judgment: \n  \n **The "search exception" dissolved under inspection.** When you itemize what's actually search-specific, it's two things — the FTS seed scan (the structural replay gate can't evaluate NIP-50 query text) and relay-pinned demand — and both are exactly what a concept owner is *supposed* to supply as parameters. The other ~80% of `SearchSessionRegistry` (open/replace/close, exactly-once close, reverse teardown via boxed actions) duplicates the feed side's `TeardownAction` machinery line for line. So it was never an exception; it was a custom seed strategy plus an unauthorized copy of the concept-neutral spine, with a doc comment ("intentionally search-shaped, not reusable") that grants itself a permanent waiver. That comment is the fishy part — it's a lane exempting itself from convergence, and "migrate later where it fits" is how *later* becomes *never*. \n  \n What I did: \n  \n - **Filed [#2777](https://github.com/pablof7z/nostr-multi-platform/issues/2777)** — "One concept-read lifecycle engine — no exempt lanes." It names all four current lanes (feed engine, search, group-feed's own `group_feed_sessions` map, and the deprecated escape hatch #2770 deletes), defines the engine-owns vs. concept-supplies split with your no-exceptions rule stated explicitly ("if the engine can't express a shipped read, the engine is wrong, not the read"), and sequences it: extract the spine from the feed machinery (the existing engine-agnostic `TeardownAction` layer gives it a head start), prove the boundary with one #2758 read, land the other three as declarative specs, then **mandatory** migration of search and group-feed — as checkboxes, not aspirations — plus a doctrine-lint ratchet so no concept crate can grow local lifecycle machinery again. \n - **Posted the constraint on [#2758](https://github.com/pablof7z/nostr-multi-platform/issues/2758)** so whoever picks it up builds against it: the four reads are acceptance tests for the engine boundary; a PR introducing a new registry, close map, replay implementation, or teardown recipe means the engine is incomplete, and that's the bug to fix. Its body had no engine requirement at all — left alone, the fan-out would have produced lanes five through eight. \n - **Saved the decision to memory**, including the no-exceptions rationale, so future sessions don't re-bless the search waiver.
