91a86-921f2 | 2026-07-03T11:35:54Z | session:91a86fdf-624c-446e-9b38-0fb02085121f | **Spending is a durable state machine, because two things can fail independently** — the mint HTTP call and the relay publish. A per-operation journal (`Draft → MintPending → MintSettled → PublishPending → Settled`, with `Unknown`/`Failed`) means a process crash mid-operation reconciles on restart instead of double-spending. [...] ## No — don't build the wallet money-safety journal on Trellis. (The read projection already rides it.) \n  \n **The one fact that settles it:** Trellis graphs are **in-memory, per-session, and die with the process by design** — built fresh in `FeedSessionTrellisAdapter::new`, torn down on `close_scope`, with zero persistence in `trellis-core`. The wallet journal's *entire reason to exist* is surviving process death after an irreversible external effect (a mint spend). Trellis can't even represent that problem, let alone solve it. [...] - **Saga (1)** writes **pre-effect**: "about to consume proofs A,B for a mint spend." That pre-record *is* the at-most-once mechanism. Durable, money-critical. \n - **Trail (3)** writes **post-observation**: "a deletion arrived / a probe found C spent / a nutzap redeemed." Diagnostic, bounded. [...] The load-bearing correction: **you can't read 2/3 out of 1**, because the saga only knows *locally-initiated* operations — and most of what makes the wallet chaotic (inbound token arrivals, NIP-09 deletions from your *other* devices, incoming nutzaps) never touches the saga at all. So it's not a subset relationship. It's **producer/consumer**: the saga *emits facts into* the trail (`MintSettled`, `Unknown→reconciled`, `Failed`) so the trail can explain locally-caused shape changes too — but their schemas never merge (money-critical pre-effect records must not live in a diagnostic log with bounded eviction). [...] - **W10 — Restart reconciliation wiring** [S after W7]. Connect the journal's `Unknown→PublishPending→Settled` reconci