91a86-8e238 | 2026-07-03T11:35:54Z | session:91a86fdf-624c-446e-9b38-0fb02085121f | **Where Trellis legitimately stays in the picture:** the wallet **read projection** (balances, pending-op summaries keyed by correlation id) is a bounded typed projection that rides the normal session path — and if that path happens to sit on Trellis-backed reconciliation, the wallet never sees it. Read side: yes, invisibly. Money-safety journal: no. [...] And on Trellis, the question that started this: doubly moot for the trail — a timeline isn't a dependency graph, *and* ADR-0075 confines Trellis trace to dev-only tooling while "why is my wallet this shape" is a product-surface question. [...] - **Projection bounded**: `MAX_WALLET_PROJECTION_ROWS=100`, keep-last truncation (`projection.rs:117`). ✔
91a86-afbec | 2026-07-03T11:35:54Z | session:91a86fdf-624c-446e-9b38-0fb02085121f | **So the clean way to get concern 3: event-source the wallet reducer.** Make current state a fold over an ordered stream of typed facts — `TokenAdded`, `TokenDeleted{cause}`, `MintProbeReconciled{proof, verdict}`, `NutzapRedeemed`, etc. — and have each fold step retain a bounded in-memory ring of "what changed + why." Then: [...] The load-bearing correction: **you can't read 2/3 out of 1**, because the saga only knows *locally-initiated* operations — and most of what makes the wallet chaotic (inbound token arrivals, NIP-09 deletions from your *other* devices, incoming nutzaps) never touches the saga at all. So it's not a subset relationship. It's **producer/consumer**: the saga *emits facts into* the trail (`MintSettled`, `Unknown→reconciled`, `Failed`) so the trail can explain locally-caused shape changes too — but their schemas never merge (money-critical pre-effect records must not live in a diagnostic log with bounded eviction). [...] I had said "in-memory, rebuild on restart." Fable caught that the naive rebuild is weak: after restart, every pre-session cause collapses to "this is just what the relays said" — you lose exactly the explanation you wanted ("balance dropped because last session a mint probe found spent proofs"). But the durable causal tier **already exists in the protocol**: NIP-60 `kind:7376` history (which the design doc already *requires* for every balance-changing op, and `nmp-nip60/src/history_event.rs` already has the codec) plus the `del` field on token events. So: \n  \n - **Don't build a durable local trail.** `kind:7376` + `del` tags *are* the durable, cross-device, encrypted causal record. \n - **The in-memory ring is the session-local high-resolution overlay** — mint-probe verdicts, arrival provenance, saga correlation ids — the stuff too fine-grained or too transient for 7376. [...] enum WalletFact { \n     TokenAdded     { token_event: EventId, amount: Msat, mint: MintUrl, via: Provenance }, \n     TokenDeleted   { token_event: EventId, cause: DeleteCause },      // NIP-09 in, or local rollover \n     MintProbed     { proof: ProofRef, verdict: ProofVerdict },        // Spent / Unspent / Unknown \n     NutzapRedeemed { nutzap: EventId, amount: Msat, sender: PubkeyRef }, \n     SagaTransition { op: CorrelationId, from: OpState, to: OpState },  // producer wiring from concern 1 \n     StateRebuilt   { from: Vec<EventId> },                            // genesis fact after restart \n } [...] - **The ring** (time-ordered, bounded) → answers *"what sequence produced this shape?"* \n - **A per-atom last-cause index** (`token_event_id → last WalletFact that touched it`) → answers *"why is **this specific** proof/token here?"* This is O(current state), not O(traffic), so a nutzap flood can't evict the cause of a token you still hold. Fable's addition — and it's the half of your question the ring alone can't answer. [...] 1. **The reducer must be confluent** — terminal state order-*insensitive* (a delete arriving before its token must tombstone, not no-op), even though the trail is order-*sensitive*. Otherwise two devices show different balances and the trail "explains" a state that shouldn't exist. \n 2. **The ring is never a rebuild authority** — restart rebuilds from Nostr + saga reconcile, entering as a `StateRebuilt` genesis fact. (Name it explicitly so nobody later "optimizes" restart into ring-replay.) [...] 4. **Privacy at the type level** — fact payloads carry event ids, op ids, amounts-by-unit, and canonical mint URLs only when already public via `kind:10019`; never proofs or secrets. Bake it into the types, not a display-edge filter. [...] - File split: saga.rs / fact.rs / trail.rs / ledger.rs (+ledger_state.rs, split later purely for the 500-LOC hard cap). \n - `TokenAdded` carries `proofs: Vec<ProofAtom>` not a flat amount, so the reducer owns proof-set membership, not just token-event membership. \n - Saga emits an explicit `WalletSagaEvent` from `transition()` (not a callback/drain) — one-directional producer wiring into `WalletFact::from`. \n - `rebuild_from` is a distinct entry point from `apply()`, folding seeds into one `StateRebuilt` genesis fact. \n - Privacy test: exhaustive-match + sealed marker trait over field types, not a runtime-only check. [...] - Mint-probe-found-spent: `mint_probe_found_spent_excludes_only_that_proof_from_balance` (+ `mint_probe_spent_verdict_is_absorbing`, added after codex found Spent wasn't monotonic). [...] Assistant: The money-critical logic is correct: `apply_token_live` refuses to revive a tombstoned token (delete-before-add stays dead), `apply_token_tombstone` is idempotent and pre-add-safe, and `apply_mint_probe` makes `Spent` absorbing (a stale `Unspent` can't resurrect balance). Both fold and rebuild route through the same guards — confluence holds. `WalletDerivedState` is deliberately non-`Serialize` (durable tier = kind:7375/7376). I'm confident in this.
