dcc80-32aeb | 2026-07-04T17:43:31Z | session:dcc80382-bcc0-45ea-8b9c-1a2fc741f872 | <teammate-message teammate_id="feed-empty-diagnosis" color="red" summary="Account-reversion CONFIRMED as root cause; filed NMP#2932"> \n RECONCILED. The account-reversion theory is CONFIRMED; the mute-difference theory stays REFUTED. chirp#39 (empty feed) and chirp#40 (account reversion) are the SAME bug: the active account reverting to None is the CAUSE, the empty feed is the EFFECT. \n  \n ## The one-variable flip (proven at pinned rev fa49d00c5) \n New test `repro_account_reversion_via_reset_empties_feed` in `crates/nmp-testing/tests/empty_home_feed_difference_repro.rs` (real live-ingest harness): \n - Sign in Alice, Alice follows Bob, Bob posts → feed shows Bob; active == Some(Alice). [N cards] \n - `reset_runtime()` then `start_runtime()` → `active_account == None`, feed == `[]`. [0 cards] \n - Re-inject Bob's note+contacts (store repopulated) while active still None → feed STILL empty. This isolates the variable: it's active-account == None, NOT lost store data or torn-down feed state. \n - Re-sign-in Alice → feed recovers Bob. \n  \n So active present ↔ None flips N ↔ 0 cards, independent of store contents AND feed composition. This is the crux you asked for. (The mute-difference flip does NOT move the needle — difference(follows, absent-mute) still shows Bob.) \n  \n ## The NMP mechanism (real latent bug) — filed NMP#2932 (p1) \n `LifecycleCommand::Reset` (cmd_lifecycle.rs:144-159) rebuilds the kernel with empty `active_account` and intentionally nulls the shared slot, but does NOT touch `IdentityRuntime` — so `IdentityRuntime::active` survives as Some. The following `Start` → `restore_active_session` early-returns at session_persistence.rs:61 (`if identity.active_pubkey().is_some() { return }`), so it NEVER re-syncs the account into the rebuilt kernel. Result: kernel/slot stay None while identity thinks it's signed in — silent desync, no crash, no restart. Exactly the chirp#40 symptom. Minimal failing test attached to the issue. \n  \n ## Is the reversion spontaneous? No — and that matters \n Verified (lifecycle trace + the agent's audit): NMP has NO autonomous/async/timer path that clears the active account. The slot only goes None via an explicit host command: `Reset`, `RemoveAccount`, or `AddSigner(AppManagedLocalNsec)` of the currently-active pubkey (runtime.rs:212-214). So on device, Chirp is issuing one of these post-sign-in. \n  \n Chirp evidence: `KernelModel+Lifecycle.swift::resetAndRestart()` does `kernel.reset()` then `kernel.start()` — precisely the desync sequence. I did NOT find a live caller of `resetAndRestart()` on the iOS branch, but the Android agent OBSERVED the reversion, so a trigger exists on that platform (reset()+start(), or an app-managed signer re-registration, or removeAccount). That last-mile trigger is Chirp-side. \n  \n ## Verdict \n - ROOT CAUSE: active-account reverting to None empties the home feed. chirp#39 = effect of chirp#40; one bug. \n - NMP side: genuine latent desync (Reset+Start doesn't restore active) → filed NMP#2932 with the minimal failing test. Recommended fix: don't gate `restore_active_session` solely on `identity.active_pubkey().is_some()` — after a kernel rebuild, re-`set_accounts` from the surviving identity (or reset identity in `reset()`, or re-sync the rebuilt kernel). \n - Chirp side: find who calls `reset()`+`start()` (or removeAccount / app-managed re-add of the active signer) after sign-in and stop it — or ensure it's always followed by an active-account restore. This is the device trigger; the framework fix (#2932) makes it non-fatal even if it fires. [...] ## The NMP mechanism (real latent bug) — filed NMP#2932 (p1) \n `LifecycleCommand::Reset` (cmd_lifecycle.rs:144-159) rebuilds the kernel with empty `active_account` and intentionally nulls the shared slot, but does NOT touch `IdentityRuntime` — so `IdentityRuntime::active` survives as Some. The following `Start` → `restore_active_session` early-returns at session_persistence.rs:61 (`if identity.active_pubkey().is_some() { return }`), so it NEVER re-syncs the account into the rebuilt kernel. Result: kernel/slot stay None while identity thinks it's signed in — silent desync, no crash, no restart. Exactly the chirp#40 symptom. Minimal failing test attached to the issue. \n  \n ## Is the reversion spontaneous? No — and that matters \n Verified (lifecycle trace + the agent's audit): NMP has NO autonomous/async/timer path that clears the active account. The slot only goes None via an explicit host command: `Reset`, `RemoveAccount`, or `AddSigner(AppManagedLocalNsec)` of the currently-active pubkey (runtime.rs:212-214). So on device, Chirp is issuing one of these post-sign-in. \n  \n Chirp evidence: `KernelModel+Lifecycle.swift::resetAndRestart()` does `kernel.reset()` then `kernel.start()` — precisely the desync sequence. I did NOT find a live caller of `resetAndRestart()` on the iOS branch, but the Android agent OBSERVED the reversion, so a trigger exists on that platform (reset()+start(), or an app-managed signer re-registration, or removeAccount). That last-mile trigger is Chirp-side. \n  \n ## Verdict \n - ROOT CAUSE: active-account reverting to None empties the home feed. chirp#39 = effect of chirp#40; one bug. \n - NMP side: genuine latent desync (Reset+Start doesn't restore active) → filed NMP#2932 with the minimal failing test. Recommended fix: don't gate `restore_active_session` solely on `identity.active_pubkey().is_some()` — after a kernel rebuild, re-`set_accounts` from the surviving identity (or reset identity in `reset()`, or re-sync the rebuilt kernel). \n - Chirp side: find who calls `reset()`+`start()` (or removeAccount / app-managed re-add of the active signer) after sign-in and stop it — or ensure it's always followed by an active-account restore. This is the device trigger; the framework fix (#2932) makes it non-fatal even if it fires.
dcc80-d50a4 | 2026-07-04T17:43:34Z | session:dcc80382-bcc0-45ea-8b9c-1a2fc741f872 | Fixed chirp#51 Bug 2. PR: https://github.com/pablof7z/chirp/pull/55 (branch chirp-ios-signer-banner, worktree /Users/pablofernandez/Work/chirp/.wt-signer-banner). \n  \n Root cause: AccountsView.swift gated the "Signer relay" health section only on `model.signerState != nil`. That's a kernel-owned GLOBAL slot (`nmp_core::actor::commands::identity::runtime::IdentityRuntime::signer_state`, written from crates/nmp-core/src/actor/commands/identity/signer_state.rs) that is never scoped or cleared per active account. A background NIP-46 bunker reconnect/handshake for a superseded or unrelated account (e.g. restored from a persisted session on cold start) can write into that slot and render against whatever account happens to be active — including a plain local-key account with no remote signer at all. \n  \n Fix (apps/ios/Chirp/Features/AccountsView.swift): additionally gate the section on the active account's own `signerIsRemote` field (`AccountSummary.signer_is_remote`), which Rust already exposes precisely for this purpose per its own doc comment. Now the section only renders when the active account actually has a remote signer. \n  \n Validated on iPhone 16 Simulator with NMP_TEST_NSEC (local-key-only sign-in): \n - Before: reproduced the bug class live — Settings > Accounts showed "Signer relay: Connected" for the "Local key" account (docs/validation/ios-signer/01-before-local-key-false-banner.jpg). Note: this run's stale value was "Connected" rather than the originally-reported "Connection failed", but it's the identical defect (a signer-health section appearing at all for a local-key account). \n - After: same session shows no signer-relay section (docs/validation/ios-signer/02-after-local-key-no-banner.jpg). \n - Real-error path: verified by reading the diff rather than a live bunker-failure repro — the fix only adds one extra `&&` condition to the existing gate; SignerStateRow and every flag it reads (isReady/isAwaitingApproval/isReconnecting/isUnavailable/isFailed) are untouched, so a genuine remote-signer account still renders exactly as before. \n  \n Filed a genuine framework gap: pablof7z/nostr-multi-platform#2976 — signer_state has no per-account identity/keying at all, so even after this fix, two remote-signer accounts could still cross-contaminate each other's health (out of scope for a Chirp-side patch; documented root cause + repro + suggested fix in the issue).
dcc80-0ec72 | 2026-07-04T17:43:38Z | session:dcc80382-bcc0-45ea-8b9c-1a2fc741f872 | TUI RESULT: the real pure-Rust Chirp shell RENDERS Bob's notes. Per your decision tree, that means the bug is DEVICE-FFI-SPECIFIC (Android JNI / iOS Swift), NOT NMP and NOT the pure-Rust shell. I proved the full pure-Rust path end-to-end at chirp master's pinned NMP rev (fa49d00c — the exact device rev): \n  \n 1. REAL C-ABI + compose + difference + decode (NEW test I added: `crates/nmp-app-chirp/src/ffi/tests/home_feed_pull.rs::chirp_difference_home_feed_surfaces_followed_author_notes`). It: `nmp_app_chirp_register` (full `compose_chirp_runtime`) → `sign_in(ALICE)` → seed store with Alice's kind:3 following Bob + Bob's 3 kind:1 notes (NO mute) → open the EXACT `Difference(active_user().follows(), list_members(ACTIVE_MUTE))` RootIndexed home feed through `nmp_app_open_feed` (JSON-marshaled FeedParams, the device path) → `decode_op_feed_snapshot`. PASSES — all 3 of Bob's followed non-self notes surface. So the C-ABI, JSON marshaling, compose, difference resolution, pull, and decode ALL work in the real shell. \n 2. RENDER half proven by the shell's own existing tests: `apps/tui/src/app/dynamic_feed_tests.rs:122` applies a home-feed frame under `CHIRP_TIMELINE_HOME_KEY` and asserts it renders into `state.rows` (`apply_nmp_event` → `SharedSnapshot::from_transport_payload` → `apply_feed_snapshot` → rows). Green. \n 3. EMISSION half proven earlier: the engine emits the frame carrying the cards with an advancing env.rev. \n  \n So engine → C-ABI → compose → difference → decode → render all work in pure Rust. The tui would show the cards.
dcc80-47223 | 2026-07-04T18:00:34Z | session:dcc80382-bcc0-45ea-8b9c-1a2fc741f872 | Not NMP follow-set reactivity. The strongest device-side explanations, in order — please have the Android agent check these: \n  \n A. SUPERSEDED SEEDED kind:3 (fits "8 received / 7 stored" exactly). Chirp's account-creation path publishes its OWN kind:3 with `nmp_chirp_config::chirp_default_follows()` (`create_account.rs:68` → `app.create_account(profile, relays, follows, ...)`) at created_at≈now. That NEWER kind:3 SUPERSEDES the seeded historical kind:3 (older created_at) → the app follows the DEFAULT set (which won't include the test author) → 0 cards, and the seeded kind:3 is the "received-but-not-stored" 1-of-8. This fires on account CREATION, not nsec sign-in — so the key question: did the emulator flow CREATE an account or SIGN IN with the seeded account's existing nsec? If create, this is almost certainly it. \n B. Author/key mismatch: the stored kind:3's author ≠ the active pubkey, or its p-tags don't contain the note author's pubkey exactly (hex vs npub), so follows resolves to a set excluding the author. \n C. Benign: a replaceable supersede among the seeds (two kind:10002, or kind:0) accounts for the 1 drop but not the empty feed. \n  \n ## Three concrete device diagnostics (decisive) \n 1. Dump the STORED kind:3: is `author == signed-in pubkey` AND do its p-tags contain the note author's pubkey (exact hex)? If either is false → that's the cause (seeding/supersede), not NMP. \n 2. Does the derived REQ `{"kinds":[1..],"authors":[<active>,<author>]}` actually go out on device? YES → follows resolved, bug is downstream (composition/decode/routing); NO → follows didn't resolve → the stored kind:3 isn't the active account's or got superseded (hypothesis A/B). \n 3. Grep the relay's received frames for a kind:3 authored by the active account with created_at≈now — if present, Chirp published a default kind:3 that clobbered the seed (hypothesis A → Chirp-side fix: don't publish default follows over a seeded/imported account, or seed the kind:3 with a future created_at). \n  \n ## Honest bottom line \n The device feed CAN render — NMP admits a followed author's note whenever the active account's kind:3 (stored or live) lists that author, at every ordering I tested. There is no NMP follow-set-reactivity gap. The remaining 0-cards is a Chirp-side seeding/lifecycle issue, most likely a default-follows kind:3 superseding the seed (A) or an author/key mismatch (B). No NMP issue to file for this layer. Tests are in the same worktree file (`empty_home_feed_difference_repro.rs`; each passes in isolation — combined runs flake only from the shared process-global actor, a harness artifact).
dcc80-58d97 | 2026-07-04T18:00:36Z | session:dcc80382-bcc0-45ea-8b9c-1a2fc741f872 | User: we need chirp ios to have search capability -- with full full-text search as provided by NMP as well as navvigating to any nevent, npub or NIP-AD -- file this as a gh issue on the chirp codebase and get a sonnet agent to start building it in a git worktree -- this should be a search button on the top-right toolbar -- launch an opus agent to decide the optimal UX we should aim for. \n  \n All the NMP primitives to make this work should be already available -- so if this is not trivial file as NMP bug because all this is supposed to already be easily possible. \n  \n Assistant: Search feature is filed and both agents are launched, in parallel with the ongoing validation sweep: \n  \n **Filed [chirp#71](https://github.com/pablof7z/chirp/issues/71)** — top-right toolbar search button doing full-text NIP-50 search *and* entity navigation (nevent→thread, npub→profile, NIP-AD→resolve). \n  \n **Two agents running:** \n - **Opus** → designing the optimal search UX (entry pattern, entity-vs-freetext disambiguation, result rows, resolve/loading/error states, accessibility) as a buildable spec comment on the issue. \n - **Sonnet** → building it in an isolated worktree, and critically **auditing primitive exposability first**: the NMP Rust primitives (`open_search`/NIP-50, ref resolution, `nmp-nip-ad`) all exist, but `open_search` isn't yet exposed to the Swift facade (that's exactly why chirp#27 removed search). So its first job is to find the sanctioned binding path — and per your instruction, **if any of the three isn't cleanly exposable to Swift, it files an NMP bug rather than hacking around it**, since this is supposed to be trivially possible. It builds what's exposable, files for what isn't, validates each path on the sim with screenshots. \n  \n I grounded the issue and both briefs in the actual code so the "primitives already exist" claim is verified, not assumed — the real question the Sonnet agent will answer is whether the *Swift exposure* of search is trivial (Chirp facade work) or a genuine NMP binding gap. \n  \n The validation sweep continues undisturbed alongside this (different worktrees; the search build creates its own sim if the sweep sims are busy). I'll report the UX spec, the exposability verdict (Chirp-side vs NMP-bug), and the built-and-validated search feature as they land.
dcc80-42518 | 2026-07-04T18:00:54Z | session:dcc80382-bcc0-45ea-8b9c-1a2fc741f872 | <teammate-message teammate_id="feed-empty-diagnosis" color="red" summary="FIXED #2944: app-owned projection_rev frozen at 0 froze rev-aware host caches. PR #2952"> \n FIXED end-to-end. PR #2952: https://github.com/pablof7z/nostr-multi-platform/pull/2952 (Closes #2944). \n  \n ## Root cause (this IS the device bug, and it's NMP) \n Running the real tui shell confirmed the pure-Rust stack renders (real C-ABI `nmp_app_open_feed` + `compose_chirp_runtime` + `Difference(follows,mute)` + `decode_op_feed_snapshot` all surface the notes). So the break is host-side — and I found it: BOTH the generated iOS `ProjectionCache.generated.swift:177` and the Android `ProjectionCache.kt:194` (the NMP-owned rev-aware host apply caches) skip a `Changed` row when `incomingRev <= cached.rev`. App-owned keys like `chirp.timeline.home` emit `projection_rev = 0` on EVERY tick (they're absent from the kernel's builtin rev manifest — `rung2_stamp.rs:31-33`). So the host commits the FIRST (empty) home-feed frame at rev 0, then skips every later card-bearing frame (`0 <= 0`) → frozen empty while the envelope rev (your "decodedRev") climbs to 26. That is exactly "decodedRev advanced to 26 yet no card." \n  \n I was wrong in layers 4/5 to call projection_rev=0 "inert" — it's inert for the Rust state-based merge cache, but FATAL for the rev-aware host caches. The kernel was violating its own `projection_rev` wire contract (ADR-0070 Rung 2: rev advances on content change) for app-owned keys. \n  \n ## The fix (single wire-contract fix → all platforms) \n `ProjectionRevTracker::stamp_app_owned_revs`: for app-owned (non-manifest) keys, derive a content-driven rev — a per-key counter that increments when the payload fingerprint changes — so the rev advances iff content changed. Cleared rows keep 0; Tier-2 built-ins untouched. Now `chirp.timeline.home` emits rev 1 (empty) → rev 2 (cards), so the host guard `2 <= 1` is false → it decodes the card frame → renders. Fixes iOS + Android + web at once (no per-platform host patch needed). \n  \n ## Verification \n - Reproducing integration test (`op_feed_projection_rev.rs`) asserts on the REAL emitted frame bytes that the home-feed projection_rev strictly advances empty→cards. FAILED before the fix (rev 0/0), PASSES after (rev 1→2). \n - Unit tests in `app_owned.rs` (advances on change, stable otherwise, Cleared=0, independent per key). \n - Full `nmp-core` lib suite (1837) + `doctrine_lint_smoke` (210) green. File-size gate clean (update.rs held at 499 via folding into `stamp_typed_projections`). \n  \n ## Notes \n - This supersedes NMP#2943 (which I'd filed as p3-inert) — #2944 IS that bug, now fixed. Suggest closing #2943 as a dup or letting #2952 close both. \n - The chirp regression test I wrote earlier (`chirp_difference_home_feed_surfaces_followed_author_notes`, real C-ABI shell) is still uncommitted in the chirp master worktree — it's a good guard but uses the recompute accessor so it doesn't catch the rev bug; the NMP `op_feed_projection_rev.rs` (emitted-frame) is the one that does. Want me to commit the chirp guard too, or drop it? \n - Once #2952 merges and NMP is re-pinned, the device home feed should render. No host-side (Swift/Kotlin) change required — but if you want belt-and-suspenders, the host caches could also treat rev==0 as always-decode; not needed with this fix. [...] <teammate-message teammate_id="projrev-fix-review" color="yellow" summary="PR #2952 verdict: APPROVE-WITH-NITS, mergeable"> \n VERDICT: APPROVE-WITH-NITS (doc-only nits; nothing blocking). The fix is correct, non-regressing for the emission ladder, and the emitter is the right layer. CI is fully green (all 18 checks, including file-size gate and all nextest suites). Merge it. \n  \n Findings on the six questions: \n  \n 1. CORRECTNESS vs contract — SOUND. Note the ADR situation first: post-ADR-0073 reset, docs/decisions/0070-typed-read-sessions.md no longer contains any Rung/projection_rev text, and ADR-0055 no longer exists on disk; the durable rev contract now lives in the emission-ladder code itself (crates/nmp-core/src/kernel/projection_rev/mod.rs header + rung2_stamp.rs). That contract: monotonic u64 per key, advances when content changes, Rung 3 omits Unchanged. Built-ins derive rev from SourceVersions write-chokepoint counters ("option C — NOT content-hash-as-gate", mod.rs:25-37). The PR uses a content-fingerprint gate for app-owned keys — a deliberate divergence, and the right one: the kernel has zero write-chokepoint visibility into host-registered projections (opaque payload bytes from run_typed_projections, update.rs:289), so content is the only available signal. It's actually a tighter approximation of "content changed" than the built-ins' source-sum. Both mechanisms satisfy the property hosts depend on: rev never stays flat across a content change. \n  \n 2. EMISSION LADDER — NO REGRESSION, strictly better. Rung 3 (kernel/update/rung3_omit.rs:63-70) decides omission from MANIFEST PRESENCE, never from entry.projection_rev; keys absent from the manifest default to Changed and are "always kept, never omitted (D3-7)". App-owned keys can never enter the manifest (build_manifest at projection_rev/mod.rs:392 enumerates exactly KERNEL_BUILTIN_PROJECTION_KEYS, test-enforced by build_manifest_covers_all_builtin_keys). So wire emission counts for app-owned keys are byte-for-byte unchanged; the only behavioral delta is host-side: unchanged payloads now keep their prior rev so rev-aware hosts correctly skip re-decode (an improvement — before the bug, they wrongly skipped everything; the naive alternative would wastefully re-decode every tick since app-owned rows are emitted every tick). \n  \n 3. OTHER CONSUMERS — none affected. The only rev-GATING consumers in the tree are the generated Swift/Kotlin ProjectionCaches (nmp-codegen/src/swift_projection_cache.rs, kotlin_projection_cache) — the exact caches this fixes. The web runtime and gallery only touch projectionRev in generated FlatBuffers decode accessors (no gating). The keyed caches (refs.profile/refs.event) ride the manifest path — Tier-2, untouched. No code anywhere treats rev==0 as a sentinel (grep clean; hits are FlatBuffers defaults). projection_emission.rs's emit_rev is a separate frame-level counter, unrelated. \n  \n 4. FINGERPRINT MECHANISM — sound. DefaultHasher over payload bytes is deterministic in-process; per-key (u64 rev, u64 fp) slots in a HashMap keyed by projection key — correctly scoped, no cross-key sharing (test-covered). Kernel is a single-threaded actor with &mut self, so no concurrency hazard. Monotonic for the kernel's lifetime: the tracker is created once (kernel_new.rs:235) and app_owned_revs is never reset (reset_last_emitted doesn't touch it — correct). The `rev == 0 || last_fp != fp` bump means a Changed row never carries rev 0, and A→B→A content toggles keep advancing (1,2,3). The Cleared→Changed edge is safe: Cleared rows skip the slot (keep rev 0 on the wire), and the host cache REMOVES the entry on Cleared (swift_projection_cache.rs:236-238 doc: "Cleared row → remove from cache"), so a subsequent Changed at an unchanged rev is admitted (cache[key] is nil). Epoch/session change: host caches do a mandatory reset (D4, swift_projection_cache.rs:224) while kernel revs persist — safe in both directions. Residual risks are negligible: a 64-bit hash collision between consecutive distinct payloads would delay one frame until the next change (~2^-64, same risk class as every fingerprint gate already in this file, e.g. diagnostics_payload_fingerprint); the map never prunes Cleared keys (tiny tuples, bounded by distinct feed keys opened per session — and keeping them is the monotonicity-safe choice if a host ever misses a Cleared). \n  \n 5. RIGHT LAYER — yes, emitter. The generated host caches implement the documented contract correctly; the kernel was violating it. Fixing the emitter corrects iOS, Android, and any future rev-aware host at once, with no regeneration of host code needed. A host-side rev==0-always-decode guard would (a) enshrine a sentinel that's not part of the contract, (b) need duplicating in every host cache, (c) mask future emitter bugs, and (d) force re-decode of app-owned payloads on EVERY tick (they're unconditionally emitted each tick), reinstating the decode churn the rev dedup exists to prevent. No defensive host guard is warranted — decode-before-commit (D3-4) already protects against bad payloads. The one scenario the emitter fix doesn't cover is an old pinned NMP under a rev-aware host, which is just the normal re-pin flow: chirp needs a re-pin to actually get this fix on device. \n  \n 6. SCOPE/FOLDING — clean. update.rs itself changed by ONE line (update.rs:396, threading &mut self.projection_rev_tracker); all new logic lives in a new dedicated module crates/nmp-core/src/kernel/projection_rev/app_owned.rs (141 lines, unit-tested) and is invoked from inside rung2_stamp::stamp_typed_projections — which is also the architecturally correct home (rev stamping is Rung 2's job), not just a file-size dodge. The test-helper call site was updated in lockstep so test and production paths stay identical. The integration regression test (nmp-testing/tests/op_feed_projection_rev.rs) asserts on real emitted frame bytes decoded exactly as a host does, and would have failed pre-fix — genuinely load-bearing. \n  \n NITS (non-blocking, one-line doc fixes): \n - rung3_omit.rs:11-12 still says rung2_stamp is "a single pure function with no side-effects on kernel state" — no longer true (it now takes &mut ProjectionRevTracker). Update the sentence. \n - projection_rev/mod.rs:25-28 header still declares the design is "NOT content-hash-as-gate"; the new sibling module is exactly that (justifiably, for opaque app-owned payloads). One clause scoping that statement to Tier-2 built-ins would prevent the header reading as contradicting app_owned.rs. \n - Optional: a comment on app_owned_revs noting Cleared entries are deliberately never pruned (monotonicity over memory) would preempt a future "cleanup" regression. \n </teammate-message>
