— flagging in case you want it looked at separately. \n  \n Important gap I found and fixed beyond the re-pin: the production `vite build` reported success but was silently broken. Vite copies @nmpis/runtime-web's wasm-loader JS into dist/assets/ verbatim (it's only reached through a runtime-computed import()), but never follows THAT module's own internal `new URL(...)` references — so the actual wasm binary and SQLite vendor snippets never landed in dist/. A real prod build would fetch the wasm, silently get the SPA index.html back instead (200 OK, wrong content-type), and hang forever on "Connecting" — confirmed this with a `vite preview` + Playwright screenshot before/after. Fixed with a `closeBundle` plugin in vite.config.ts that stages the package's dist/wasm/ tree into dist/assets/ post-build; re-verified with preview+Playwright that it now reaches "connected" same as dev. \n  \n Also found apps/web/vercel.json, scripts/build.sh, and the build:wasm/build:vercel npm scripts still assumed the pre-split monorepo layout (crates/nmp-browser-runtime, pkg/nmp-browser-runtime, a root web/ workspace) that doesn't exist in the split chirp repo — that pipeline was dead on arrival for any real Vercel deploy, unrelated to my change. Removed the dead script + npm scripts, repointed vercel.json at the working `npm install` + `npm run build` path, and updated docs/chirp-web.md's Production Deployment Contract to describe the packages-based reality instead of the stale monorepo-source-build contract. This is consumer-repo work so I fixed it directly rather than filing it. \n  \n No NMP framework issues needed — everything blocking a real run was fixable in Chirp. Bottom line: Chirp web now genuinely works end-to-end (dev and real production build) against the published NMP packages.
