dcc80-f11fc | 2026-07-04T18:27:27Z | session:dcc80382-bcc0-45ea-8b9c-1a2fc741f872 | <teammate-message teammate_id="chirp-ios-dm-fix" color="red" summary="DM send confirmed working over real wss relay"> \n DM send DOES succeed against a real relay — confirmed with a full two-device round trip. Here's what I did and found: \n  \n **Setup**: seeded two identities (A, B) with kind:0/10002/10050 on wss://nos.lol (a real production relay), with kind:10050 relay tags correctly using wss:// (unlike nak serve, which can only produce ws://). \n  \n **Send side**: signed in as A on the iPhone 16 sim, opened a DM to B (first-time contact, no prior interaction), sent two messages. Both succeeded with no error toast. Confirmed via `nak req -k 1059 -p <B_pubkey> wss://nos.lol`: two kind:1059 gift-wrap events p-tagging B (one per message), plus matching self-copy gift-wraps p-tagging A. Screenshot: docs/validation/ios-dm/02-dm-send-success-real-wss-relay.jpg. \n  \n **Receive side**: booted a second, independent simulator (29er-M001-fresh-uat), signed in as B, pointed at the same relay. Both messages showed up in B's Chats list, correctly decrypted, with A's real profile name ("alice-wss-test") resolved as the sender. Screenshot: docs/validation/ios-dm/03-dm-received-decrypted-on-device-b.jpg. \n  \n This is a genuine two-way, cross-device, real-relay confirmation — not just "giftwrap landed on relay" but "second identity independently received and decrypted it." It fully confirms the DM code path (Kind10050Parser, DmRelayCache, gift-wrap publish, and recipient-side decrypt/ingest) is correct end-to-end. The earlier nak-serve failure really was 100% the plaintext/TLS mismatch — nothing else needed fixing.
